\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Two free staggered transfer constructions}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Sharatchandra, Thun and Weisz set gauge fields aside
at the start of their transfer construction. They assert
that including them introduces no new features; that
sentence supplies no gauged proof
\citet{Section 4, p. 220}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
On a finite lattice with specified boundary conditions,
they construct a Fock-space and Grassmann-symbol relation
\citet{(4.3)-(4.6), p. 221}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
This is additional operator-to-symbol data, rather than an
equality of ordinary products.}

\p{When both one-component field types occupy every
site, auxiliary Grassmann variables enter their transfer
representation. Its one-step operator is Hermitian and
has negative eigenvalues; its square is positive and
advances two fine temporal spacings
\citet{p. 222; (4.10), (4.12)-(4.13), p. 223}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
The alternate-site construction changes the field
placement and has a Hermitian positive definite transfer
operator \citet{(4.14)-(4.17), pp. 223-224}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
These are distinct transfer results. A correspondence
with #{\extD=\extd+\extdag} requires an actual operator
intertwiner, together with matching fields and boundaries.}
